.TH DIRECTORY 3 "August 1, 1992"
.UC 5
.SH NAME
opendir, readdir, rewinddir, closedir \- directory operations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft B
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/dir.h>  \fR/* POSIX applications \fB#include <dirent.h>\fR */
.PP
.ft B
DIR *opendir(const char *\fIdirname\fP);
.PP
.ft B
struct direct *readdir(DIR *\fIdirp\fP);
.ft R
    /* Returns \fBstruct dirent *\fP for POSIX applications. */
.PP
.ft B
void rewinddir(DIR *\fIdirp\fP);
.PP
.ft B
int closedir(DIR *\fIdirp\fP);
.ft R
.fi
.SH (ALSO AVAILABLE IN BSD)
.nf
.ft B
long telldir(DIR *\fIdirp\fP);
.PP
.ft B
int seekdir(DIR *\fIdirp\fP, long \fIloc\fP);
.ft R
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.IR opendir 
function opens the directory named by \fIdirname\fP
and associates a \fIdirectory stream\fP with it.
The function returns a pointer to be used to identify the
directory stream in subsequent operations.
The directory stream is positioned at the first entry of \fIdirname\fP.
A NULL pointer is returned if \fIdirname\fP
can not be accessed, or if it can not
.IR malloc 
enough memory to hold the whole thing.
.PP
The
.IR readdir 
function returns a pointer to a
.I direct
structure (
.I dirent
for POSIX applications)
representing the directory entry at the current position in the
directory stream referred to by \fIdirp\fP,
and positions the directory stream at the next entry.
It returns a NULL pointer upon reaching the end of the directory stream.
.PP
The
.IR direct /\fIdirent\fP
structure includes the following member:
.RS
.TP 35
\fBMember Type	Member Name\fP
\fBDescription\fP
.TP 35
\fIchar\fP[]		\fId_name\fP
Null-terminated filename.
.RE
.PP
The
.IR rewinddir 
function resets the position of the directory stream referred to by
\fIdirp\fP to the beginning of the directory.
It does not return a value.
.PP
The
.IR closedir 
function closes the directory stream referred to by
\fIdirp\fP and frees the structure.
.PP
The following sample code searches the current working directory for
directory entry ``name'':
.IP
.PP
.br
	len = strlen(name);
.br
	dirp = opendir(".");
.br
	for (dp = readdir(dirp); dp != NULL; dp = readdir(dirp)) {
.br
		if (dp->d_namlen == len && !strcmp(dp->d_name, name)) {
.br
			closedir(dirp);
.br
			return FOUND;
.br
		}
.br
	}
.br
	closedir(dirp);
.br
	return NOT_FOUND;
.IP
.PP
.I Telldir
returns the current location associated with the named
.I directory stream.
.PP
.I Seekdir
sets the position of the next
.I readdir
operation on the
.I directory stream.
The new position reverts to the one associated with the
.I directory stream
when the
.I telldir
operation was performed.  Values returned by
.I telldir
are good only for the lifetime of the DIR pointer from which they are derived.
If the directory is closed and then reopened, the 
.I telldir
value may be invalidated due to undetected directory compaction.
It is safe to use a previous
.I telldir
value immediately after a call to
.I opendir
and before any calls to
.I readdir.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
Upon successful completion,
.IR opendir 
returns a pointer to a \fIDIR\fP structure.
Otherwise, a NULL
pointer is returned and \fIerrno\fP
is set to indicate the error.
.PP
Upon successful completion,
.IR readdir 
returns a pointer to a \fIdirect\fP/\fIdirent\fP structure.
When an error occurs, a NULL pointer is returned and \fIerrno\fP
is set to indicate the error.
When the end of the directory stream is reached, a NULL
pointer is returned and \fIerrno\fP is left unchanged.
.PP
Upon successful completion,
.IR closedir 
returns zero.  Otherwise, \-1 is returned and \fIerrno\fP
is set to indicate the error.
.SH "ERRORS"
If any of the following conditions occurs, the
.IR opendir 
function returns a NULL pointer and sets \fIerrno\fP
to the corresponding value:
.TP 15
[EACCES]
Search permission is denied for a component of the path prefix of
.IR dirname ,
or read permission is denied for the directory itself.
.TP 15
[ENAMETOOLONG]
A component of
.I direname
exceeds 255 characters,
or the entire
.I dirname
exceeds 1023 characters.
For POSIX applications these values are given
by the constants {NAME_MAX} and {PATH_MAX}, respectively.
.TP 15
[ENOENT]
The directory named by \fIdirname\fP does not exist, or the argument
points to an empty string.
.TP
[ENOTDIR]
A pathname component of \fIdirname\fP is not a directory.
.IP
.PP
If any of the following conditions occurs, the
.IR readdir 
function returns a NULL pointer and sets \fIerrno\fP
to the corresponding value:
.TP 15
[EBADF]
The \fIdirp\fP argument is a NULL pointer.
.IP
.PP
If any of the following conditions occurs, the
.IR closedir 
function returns \-1 and sets \fIerrno\fP
to the corresponding value:
.TP 15
[EBADF]
The \fIdirp\fP argument is a NULL pointer.
.SH "NOTES"
The
.I telldir
and
.I seekdir
functions are not POSIX-compliant.
POSIX applications should avoid using them.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
open(2),
close(2),
read(2),
lseek(2)
